%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

We define the correlation-selection gap as the disparity between a signal's instance-level rank correlation with F1 and its ability to select the best output from $N$ candidates.

Our argument proceeds in three layers.
First, four of five signals (SJ, FK, EM, VC) are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates for a given input; they \textit{cannot} rank candidates within an instance by construction, and consensus-proximity (MBR) conversion does not reliably recover per-sample ranking.
Second, MBR selection with well-aligned utility (within-instance SJ--F1 $\rho{=}0.52$) should in principle select better outputs, but instance degeneracy collapses the candidate pool and eliminates the headroom MBR would otherwise exploit.
Third, LP, the sole natively per-sample signal, requires two jointly necessary conditions for effective selection: low degeneracy to provide exploitable headroom, and adequate LP-quality alignment (within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}) \gtrsim 0.3$) to exploit it; compression (median range 0.041 nats) contributes but is not the sole bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ NER selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$, $p$ vs.\ greedy baseline from paired sign tests). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. Random: uniform random selection from $N$ candidates. EM and VC show negative direction (selection degrades over greedy); $p$-values omitted as effects are opposite to beneficial. Non-tied LP subset analysis in \S\ref{sec:discussion}; RE selection in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.778 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & --- \\
\midrule
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite moderate quality estimation, confidence signals yield limited selection gains (Table~\ref{tab:selection}, Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
The 3.5~pp gap between greedy (0.645) and random selection (0.610) reflects the difference between deterministic decoding ($T{=}0$) and uniformly sampling one of $N$ stochastic candidates ($T{=}1.0$); greedy is not drawn from the candidate pool.
Instance-level signals (SJ, FK, EM, VC) assign identical QE scores to all candidates per input; consensus-proximity (MBR) converts these to per-sample rankings (Table~\ref{tab:selection}: SJ 0.646, FK 0.646, EM 0.637, VC 0.623), but none consistently improves over the greedy baseline on the primary test bed.
The key question is whether LP, the only natively per-sample signal, can exploit the oracle headroom.

\paragraph{No significant improvement over greedy.}
On SciERC NER (2-seed sign tests\footnote{Multi-seed NER experiments confirm seed variation is small ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}).}, $n{=}529$), the oracle (0.778) reveals 13.3~pp of headroom above greedy (0.645), yet LP achieves only 0.647 ($\Delta{=}{+}0.2$~pp, $d{=}0.038$, $p{=}0.29$); the observed LP advantage is small and not reliably detected at $N{=}8$ (absence of evidence should not be equated with evidence of absence;\footnote{Post-hoc power analysis: at $n{=}529$ with 2-seed Fisher combination, detecting a 1~pp effect achieves approximately 55\% power. Few-NERD ($n{=}32{,}565$) provides ${>}99$\% power for the same effect size and serves as the definitive test.} TOST equivalence holds under all external anchors for LLaMA-CoNLL but varies by configuration, Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); RE selection results are consistent (Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}).
With $N{=}16$, LP selection on SciERC yields a paired improvement of $+0.52$~pp (bootstrap $p{=}0.003$). The signal appears to become actionable at larger candidate pools where degeneracy drops and headroom expands.
Few-NERD provides a stronger demonstration: LP achieves significant gains ($+1.36$~pp, $p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy, while structural signals still fail (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).
CoNLL's near-ceiling single-sample F1 (0.91) with only ${\sim}$4~pp oracle headroom functions primarily as a ceiling-effect control.
Under stringent external TOST anchors ($\delta_0{=}1.0$~pp), some configurations (e.g., Qwen CoNLL) show statistically significant degradation (0.9--1.5~pp below greedy); signal-based selection can be actively harmful rather than merely ineffective (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
LP approximately matches greedy decoding ($T{=}0$; $\Delta{=}{+}0.2$~pp, not significant); neither significantly outperforms the other, yet both fall far short of the oracle (0.778, 13.3~pp gap).
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best; closing this gap likely requires trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.\footnote{For negative results (all $p{>}0.05$), uncorrected tests suffice; the sole positive finding (non-tied LP subset, $+2.9$~pp, $p{=}0.004$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) survives Bonferroni correction over 5 comparisons.}

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-dataset comparison of LP selection effectiveness (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). LP selection gains are inversely associated with instance degeneracy across three datasets (full-set evaluation; see Table~\ref{tab:selection} footnote for gold-filtered protocol difference).\protect\footnotemark\protect\footnotemark}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Headroom & Degen\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 4.0\,pp & 47.4\% & .316 & fail \\
SciERC & 551 & .614 & 13.8\,pp & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.42\,pp \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 12.4\,pp & 13.8\% & .421 & $\mathbf{+1.36}$\,pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\footnotetext{SciERC greedy (0.614) and headroom (13.8~pp) differ from Table~\ref{tab:selection} (gold-filtered $n{=}529$, greedy${=}0.645$) due to full-set evaluation; the ranking and significance are unchanged.}
\footnotetext{Instance degeneracy: fraction of gold-filtered instances where all $N$ samples produce identical instance-level F1.}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) reveals that LP selection effectiveness is inversely associated with instance degeneracy across datasets.
Under standard early stopping, Few-NERD degeneracy is 13.8\% (vs.\ SciERC's 17.8\%, CoNLL's 47.4\%; training convergence modulates this rate, Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}), and LP achieves highly significant selection gains ($+1.36$~pp, 95\% CI $[+1.18, +1.53]$, $p{<}0.001$), while all other signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$, FK $-2.26$, EM $-3.85$~pp).

\paragraph{Two necessary conditions for LP selection.}
LP selection requires both low degeneracy and adequate LP-quality alignment.
The cross-dataset gradient is monotonic (47.4\% $\to$ fail, 17.8\% $\to$ $+0.42$~pp, 13.8\% $\to$ $+1.36$~pp; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), and degeneracy below ${\sim}$15\% consistently enables positive LP gains.
However, low degeneracy alone is not sufficient: the LoRA-rank ablation (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}) reduces SciERC degeneracy to 7.2\% with wider LP spread (median range 0.057 vs.\ 0.041 nats), yet LP selection still fails because within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$ remains weak.
Few-NERD, where LP succeeds, satisfies both conditions: low degeneracy (13.8\%) and strong alignment ($\rho{\approx}0.5$).
Per-type analysis within Few-NERD confirms the pattern (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\paragraph{The correlation-selection gap persists for structural signals.}
All five signals achieve positive quality estimation on Few-NERD (LP $\rho{=}0.42$, VC $0.39$, FK $0.31$, SJ $0.30$, EM $0.29$), yet only LP translates QE into selection.
The gap between VC's quality estimation ($\rho{=}0.39$) and its selection outcome ($-1.79$~pp) confirms that the gap is not an artifact of weak QE: even strong instance-level QE signals cannot rank candidates within an instance.
Within Few-NERD, 7 of 8 entity types show positive LP selection gains ($+0.23$ to $+4.73$~pp), the sole exception being the highest-degeneracy type (Art, 19.8\%; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain at two granularities. Blue circles: cross-dataset aggregates ($n{=}3$, exploratory). Orange triangles: per-entity-type within Few-NERD ($n{=}8$, Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$, controlled). The dashed line at ${\sim}$15\%\protect\footnotemark{} separates a regime where LP selection improves over greedy (left) from one where it fails (right). The cross-dataset monotonic trend is suggestive ($n{=}3$) but not statistically testable; the within-dataset entity-type evidence provides rigorous confirmation under controlled conditions.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

\footnotetext{Empirically, the boundary falls between Few-NERD Person (13.4\%, LP $+1.25$~pp) and Art (19.8\%, LP $-0.17$~pp); we use ${\sim}$15\% as an approximate characterization.}

Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter} visualizes the relationship at two granularities.
The cross-dataset monotonic trend is suggestive ($n{=}3$) but not statistically testable; the within-dataset entity-type evidence provides rigorous confirmation under controlled conditions ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$, $n{=}8$).

\paragraph{LP selection scales with sample budget in the low-degeneracy regime.}
On the same 4{,}351 Few-NERD instances, doubling $N$ from 8 to 16 yields a significant additional $+0.52$~pp (paired bootstrap $p{=}0.003$, 95\% CI $[+0.17, +0.87]$): LP selection improves from $+1.37$~pp to $+1.88$~pp (both $p{<}0.001$).
The gain is driven by reduced degeneracy (14.6\% $\to$ 7.0\%) expanding oracle headroom (12.1 $\to$ 15.4~pp), while LP ranking quality remains stable ($\rho{\approx}0.62$). Signal quality is thus model-intrinsic rather than $N$-dependent.
At $N{=}16$, all 8 entity types show positive LP gains, including types that were negative at $N{=}8$.
Structural signals remain uniformly negative at $N{=}16$ (SJ $-1.36$, FK $-1.47$~pp); the correlation-selection gap for consensus-based methods persists regardless of sample budget.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); LP shows the largest temperature sensitivity ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$).
Signal rankings are stable across seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.0$ at $N{=}8$, though statistical precision is limited with $m{=}3$ raters, exact $p{\approx}0.028$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}).
NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: Qwen3-8B vs.\ 4B NER quality estimation ($\rho$; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed${=}42$). 8B values identical to Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}; 4B uses matched evaluation protocol. RE scale ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
Repeating with Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), quality estimation is comparable or improved (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy on SciERC ($\Delta$ up to $-3.4$~pp) despite 13.0~pp of oracle headroom, and indistinguishable on CoNLL (${<}0.3$~pp, 1.7~pp headroom). Within the 3B--8B range tested across two model families, task difficulty is a stronger predictor of the gap than model scale; results hold at smaller scale (LLaMA-3.2-3B, 13.6~pp headroom; Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Instance degeneracy also scales with model capability: 8B shows higher degeneracy than 4B on SciERC (17.8\% vs.\ 10.4\%) despite comparable within-instance utility correlation (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{LoRA-rank ablation.}
To disentangle model capacity from degeneracy, we train Qwen3-8B on SciERC with LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ rank${=}32$ in all other experiments).
Reducing rank lowers degeneracy from 17.8\% to 7.2\% and increases oracle headroom from 13.3~pp to 16.0~pp; fine-tuning capacity drives output convergence.
However, selection still fails: the best signal (LP, 0.635) falls short of greedy (0.640), and all consensus-proximity signals perform worse (SJ 0.631, VC 0.592).
Quality estimation remains comparable (VC $\rho{=}0.394$ vs.\ $0.379$; SJ $\rho{=}0.365$ vs.\ $0.360$).
The ablation isolates the second condition: despite wider LP spread (median range 0.057 vs.\ 0.041 nats) and lower degeneracy, within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$ remains too weak for effective ranking. Both low degeneracy and adequate LP-quality alignment are independently necessary.

\paragraph{Supervised verifier baseline.}
To test whether the information bottleneck lies in the aggregation method or the features themselves, we train supervised verifiers on all five signals (3-fold cross-validation on SciERC, $N{=}16$, oracle headroom $+17.9$~pp).
Logistic regression achieves F1${=}0.641$ ($-0.3$~pp vs.\ greedy\footnote{Greedy F1 is 0.644 (3-seed mean in the verifier experiment), consistent with Table~\ref{tab:selection} (0.645, 2-seed mean on the gold-filtered subset).}, AUROC${=}0.652$); a 2-layer MLP achieves F1${=}0.639$ ($-0.6$~pp, AUROC${=}0.639$); a random forest achieves F1${=}0.615$ ($-2.9$~pp, AUROC${=}0.587$).
Adding 10 pairwise interaction features (15 total) to the MLP yields AUROC${=}0.632$, strictly worse than the 5-feature variant.
All supervised models perform at or below greedy despite $+17.9$~pp of oracle headroom, and no model exceeds single-signal VC (AUROC${=}0.706$).
Feature importance is dominated by VC (LR coefficient 0.65--0.92) with SJ receiving a negative coefficient.
The failure confirms the bottleneck is in what the signals encode, not how they are aggregated: nonlinear models with interaction terms cannot extract per-sample discriminative content that the features do not carry.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Self-consistency (majority voting over multiple samples) yields large gains on discrete-answer reasoning \citep{wang2023selfconsistency}, and test-time compute scaling has emerged as a complementary axis to model scaling \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys}.
But named entity recognition (NER) poses a structurally different challenge: the output is a \textit{set} of entities, two samples may extract overlapping but non-identical sets, and there is no obvious majority to vote on.

Prior work on confidence estimation for LLM-based IE has conflated \textit{quality estimation} (which inputs does the model handle reliably?) with \textit{sample selection} (which of $N$ outputs is best?).
Existing self-consistency methods \cite{wang2023selfconsistency, chen2024usc, taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} assume outputs can be grouped by equivalence, an assumption that breaks for entity sets with partial overlap.
Unlike \citet{yao2026consistency}, who demonstrate that self-consistency correlates with task accuracy, we investigate \textit{why} this correlation fails to yield selection improvements, diagnosing degeneracy and compression as the two blocking mechanisms.
Our experiments use fine-tuned models, the dominant deployment paradigm for production NER, and separate quality estimation from best-of-$N$ selection.

A methodological premise structures our analysis: four of five signals (SJ, VC, FK, EM) are instance-level aggregates that by design assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates for a given input; only log-probability (LP) is natively per-sample.
These signals achieve reliable quality estimation ($\rho$ up to 0.38 on the primary test bed, 0.42 on Few-NERD; AUROC up to 0.82; Figure~\ref{fig:overview}), supporting selective prediction and error flagging.
Yet quality estimation does not translate to structural selection, a disparity we term the \textbf{correlation-selection gap} (the disparity between a signal's ability to rank instances reliably and its failure to select the best output within a single instance): structural best-of-$N$ selection matches or degrades greedy decoding ($T{=}0$) despite 12\textendash 14~pp oracle headroom.
The gap has two empirical causes: (a)~\textit{instance degeneracy}, where fine-tuned models produce identical outputs for 14\textendash 47\% of instances, which eliminates selection headroom despite positive within-instance utility\textendash F1 correlation (median $\rho{=}0.52$); and (b)~\textit{weak LP-quality alignment}, where within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1})$ is too low to exploit the remaining headroom (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
LP selection requires both low degeneracy and adequate alignment: significant at 13.8\% degeneracy with $\rho{\approx}0.5$ ($+1.36$~pp, $p{<}0.001$), sub-significant at 17.8\% with $\rho{\approx}0.24$ ($+0.42$~pp), and absent at 47.4\%.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_overview}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap. \textbf{Left}: consistency signals reliably predict instance-level quality ($\rho$ up to 0.45 on CoNLL, 0.38 on the primary test bed, 0.42 on Few-NERD; AUROC up to 0.82). \textbf{Right}: quality estimation does not translate to selection; all methods approximately match greedy decoding ($T{=}0$) despite 13~pp oracle headroom (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We evaluate five signals from three families (structural, surface, and model-internal) on SciERC \cite{luan2018scierc} as a primary medium-difficulty test bed, with Few-NERD \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (low-degeneracy contrast), CoNLL-2003 \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (ceiling-effect control), and WNUT-17 \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (signal-collapse boundary), using two 8B models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) with a 4B ablation (Qwen3-4B).
We vary candidate pool size ($N{\in}\{2,\ldots,32\}$), signal sophistication, and model capacity within the 4B\textendash 8B range; behavior at 70B+ scales remains an open question.
An exploratory extension to relation extraction is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.
Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation framework.}
    We systematically evaluate five confidence signals from three families (structural, surface, model-internal) for LLM-based NER across four benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, two model families, and a 4B scale ablation.
    Structural signals reliably predict output quality (VC $\rho{=}0.38$ on the primary test bed; EM AUROC${=}0.82$ on CoNLL), with difficulty-dependent but model-stable rankings that support selective prediction and error flagging.

    \item \textbf{Diagnosis of the correlation-selection gap.}
    We identify a three-layer failure:
    (a)~four instance-level signals cannot rank candidates within an instance \textit{by construction};
    (b)~MBR selection with well-aligned utility (within-instance $\rho{=}0.52$) fails because instance degeneracy (14\textendash 47\% of instances yield identical outputs) collapses the candidate pool;
    (c)~LP, the sole per-sample signal, requires two jointly necessary conditions: low degeneracy and adequate LP-quality alignment ($\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}) \gtrsim 0.3$).
    A diagnostic chain (LoRA-rank ablation, free-form decoding, entity-token LP, supervised signal combination) confirms the bottleneck lies in the signal space accessible to unsupervised aggregation, not signal combination methods.

    \item \textbf{Two necessary conditions for LP selection.}
    We identify two jointly necessary conditions: (1)~low instance degeneracy and (2)~adequate LP-quality alignment ($\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}) \gtrsim 0.3$), verified through two independent control paths.
    The cross-dataset gradient (47\% $\to$ fail, 18\% $\to$ $+0.42$~pp, 14\% $\to$ $+1.36$~pp at $p{<}0.001$) establishes degeneracy as one condition; LoRA-rank reduction (degeneracy 7.2\%, LP fails because $\rho{\approx}0.24$) and temperature increase ($T{=}1.2$, degeneracy 12.5\%, $\rho{=}0.225$) independently confirm that alignment is the other.
    Entity-type-level analysis provides statistical grounding ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$, $n{=}8$); in the low-degeneracy regime, LP selection scales with $N$ ($+0.52$~pp from $N{=}8$ to $N{=}16$, paired $p{=}0.003$).
\end{enumerate}

